window.LEXQUEST_REFERENCE_FILES = {"labor":{"laws":{"art34":{"title":"Labor Code, Article 34 — Prohibited Practices","text":"ART. 34. PROHIBITED PRACTICES\nIt shall be unlawful for any individual, entity, licensee, or holder of authority:\n(a) To charge or accept, directly or indirectly, any amount greater than that specified in the schedule of allowable fees prescribed by the Secretary of Labor, or to make a worker pay any amount greater than that actually received by him as a loan or advance;\n(b) To furnish or publish any false notice or information or document in relation to recruitment or employment;\n(c) To give any false notice, testimony, information or document or commit any act of misrepresentation for the purpose of securing a license or authority under this Code;\n(d) To induce or to attempt to induce a worker already employed to quit his employment in order to offer him to another unless the transfer is designed to liberate the worker from oppressive terms and conditions of employment;\n(e) To influence or to attempt to influence any person or entity not to employ any worker who has not applied for employment through his agency;\n(f) To engage in the recruitment or placement of workers in jobs harmful to public health or morality or to the dignity of the Republic of the Philippines;\n(g) To obstruct or attempt to obstruct inspection by the Secretary of Labor or by his duly authorized representatives;\n(h) To fail to file reports on the status of employment, placement, vacancies, remittances of foreign exchange earnings, separation from jobs, departures and such other matters or information as may be required by the Secretary of Labor;\n(i) To substitute or alter employment contracts approved and verified by the Department of Labor from the time of actual signing thereof by the parties up to and including the periods of expiration of the same without the approval of the Secretary of Labor;\n(j) To become an officer or member of the Board of any corporation engaged in travel agency or to be engaged directly or indirectly in the management of a travel agency; and\n(k) To withhold or deny travel documents from applicant workers before departure for monetary or financial considerations other than those authorized under this Code and its implementing rules and regulations.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"art38":{"title":"Labor Code, Article 38 — Illegal Recruitment","text":"ART. 38. ILLEGAL RECRUITMENT\n(a) Any recruitment activities, including the prohibited practices enumerated under Article 34 of this Code, to be undertaken by non-licensees or non-holders of authority shall be deemed illegal and punishable under Article 39 of this Code. The Department of Labor and Employment or any law enforcement officer may initiate complaints under this Article.\n(b) Illegal recruitment when committed by a syndicate or in large scale shall be considered an offense involving economic sabotage and shall be penalized in accordance with Article 39 hereof. Illegal recruitment is deemed committed by a syndicate if carried out by a group of three (3) or more persons conspiring and/or confederating with one another in carrying out any unlawful or illegal transaction, enterprise or scheme defined under the first paragraph hereof. Illegal recruitment is deemed committed in large scale if committed against three (3) or more persons individually or as a group.\n(c) The Secretary of Labor and Employment or his duly authorized representatives shall have the power to cause the arrest and detention of such non-licensee or non-holder of authority if after investigation it is determined that his activities constitute a danger to national security and public order or will lead to further exploitation of job-seekers. The Minister shall order the search of the office or premises and seizure of documents, paraphernalia, properties and other implements used in illegal recruitment activities and the closure of companies, establishments and entities found to be engaged in the recruitment of workers for overseas employment, without having been licensed or authorized to do so.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"art39":{"title":"Labor Code, Article 39 — Penalties","text":"ART. 39. PENALTIES\n(a) The penalty of life imprisonment and a fine of One Hundred Thousand Pesos (P100,000) shall be imposed if illegal recruitment constitutes economic sabotage as defined herein;\n(b) Any licensee or holder of authority found violating or causing another to violate any provision of this Title or its implementing rules and regulations shall, upon conviction thereof, suffer the penalty of imprisonment of not less than two years nor more than five years or a fine of not less than P10,000 nor more than P50,000, or both such imprisonment and fine, at the discretion of the court;\n(c) Any person who is neither a licensee nor a holder of authority under this Title found violating any provision thereof or its implementing rules and regulations shall, upon conviction thereof, suffer the penalty of imprisonment of not less than four years nor more than eight years or a fine of not less than P20,000 nor more than P100,000 or both such imprisonment and fine, at the discretion of the court;\n(d) If the offender is a corporation, partnership, association or entity, the penalty shall be imposed upon the officer or officers of the corporation, partnership, association or entity responsible for violation, and if such officer is an alien, he shall, in addition to the penalties herein prescribed, be deported without further proceedings; and\n(e) In every case, conviction shall cause and carry the automatic revocation of the license or authority and all the permits and privileges granted to such person or entity under this Title, and the forfeiture of the cash and surety bonds in favor of the Overseas Employment Development Board or the National Seamen Board, as the case may be, both of which are authorized to use the same exclusively to promote their objectives.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"sec6":{"title":"R.A. No. 8042, Section 6 — Illegal Recruitment","text":"SEC. 6. Definition. — For purposes of this Act, illegal recruitment shall mean any act of canvassing, enlisting, contracting, transporting, utilizing, hiring, or procuring workers and includes referring, contract services, promising or advertising for employment abroad, whether for profit or not, when undertaken by non-licensee or non-holder of authority contemplated under Article 13(f) of Presidential Decree No. 442, as amended, otherwise known as the Labor Code of the Philippines: Provided, That any such non-licensee or non-holder who, in any manner, offers or promises for a fee employment abroad to two or more persons shall be deemed so engaged. It shall likewise include the following acts, when committed by any person, whether a non-licensee, non-holder, licensee or holder of authority:\n(a) To charge or accept directly or indirectly any amount greater than that specified in the schedule of allowable fees prescribed by the Secretary of Labor and Employment, or to make a worker pay or acknowledge any amount greater than that actually received by him as a loan or advance;\n(b) To furnish or publish any false notice or information or document in relation to recruitment or employment;\n(c) To give any false notice, testimony, information or document or commit any act of misrepresentation for the purpose of securing a license or authority under the Labor Code, or for the purpose of documenting hired workers with the POEA, which include the act of reprocessing workers through a job order that pertains to nonexistent work, work different from the actual overseas work, or work with a different employer whether registered or not with the POEA;\n(d) To induce or attempt to induce a worker already employed to quit his employment in order to offer him another unless the transfer is designed to liberate a worker from oppressive terms and conditions of employment;\n(e) To influence or attempt to influence any person or entity not to employ any worker who has not applied for employment through his agency or who has formed, joined or supported, or has contacted or is supported by any union or workers’ organization;\n(f) To engage in the recruitment or placement of workers in jobs harmful to public health or morality or to the dignity of the Republic of the Philippines;\n(g) To obstruct or attempt to obstruct inspection by the Secretary of Labor and Employment or by his duly authorized representive;\n(h) To fail to submit reports on the status of employment, placement vacancies, remittance of foreign exchange earnings, separation from jobs, departures and such other matters or information as may be required by the Secretary of Labor and Employment;\n(i) To substitute or alter to the prejudice of the worker, employment contracts approved and verified by the Department of Labor and Employment from the time of actual signing thereof by the parties up to and including the period of the expiration of the same without the approval of the Department of Labor and Employment;\n(j) For an officer or agent of a recruitment or placement agency to become an officer or member of the Board of any corporation engaged in travel agency or to be engaged directly or indirectly in the management of a travel agency;\n(k) To withhold or deny travel documents from applicant workers before departure for monetary or financial considerations, or for any other reasons, other than those authorized under the Labor Code and its implementing rules and regulations;\n(l) Failure to actually deploy a contracted worker without valid reason as determined by the Department of Labor and Employment;\n(m) Failure to reimburse expenses incurred by the worker in connection with his documentation and processing for purposes of deployment, in cases where the deployment does not actually take place without the worker’s fault. Illegal recruitment when committed by a syndicate or in large scale shall be considered an offense involving economic sabotage; and\n(n) To allow a non-Filipino citizen to head or manage a licensed recruitment/manning agency.\nIllegal recruitment is deemed committed by a syndicate if carried out by a group of three (3) or more persons conspiring or confederating with one another. It is deemed committed in large scale if committed against three (3) or more persons individually or as a group.\nIn addition to the acts enumerated above, it shall also be unlawful for any person or entity to commit the following prohibited acts:\n(1) Grant a loan to an overseas Filipino worker with interest exceeding eight percent (8%) per annum, which will be used for payment of legal and allowable placement fees and make the migrant worker issue, either personally or through a guarantor or accommodation party, postdated checks in relation to the said loan;\n(2) Impose a compulsory and exclusive arrangement whereby an overseas Filipino worker is required to avail of a loan only from specifically designated institutions, entities or persons;\n(3) Refuse to condone or renegotiate a loan incurred by an overseas Filipino worker after the latter’s employment contract has been prematurely terminated through no fault of his or her own;\n(4) Impose a compulsory and exclusive arrangement whereby an overseas Filipino worker is required to undergo health examinations only from specifically designated medical clinics, institutions, entities or persons, except in the case of a seafarer whose medical examination cost is shouldered by the principal/shipowner;\n(5) Impose a compulsory and exclusive arrangement whereby an overseas Filipino worker is required to undergo training, seminar, instruction or schooling of any kind only from specifically designated institutions, entities or persons, except for recommendatory trainings mandated by principals/shipowners where the latter shoulder the cost of such trainings;\n(6) For a suspended recruitment/manning agency to engage in any kind of recruitment activity including the processing of pending workers’ applications; and\n(7) For a recruitment/manning agency or a foreign principal/employer to pass on the overseas Filipino worker or deduct from his or her salary the payment of the cost of insurance fees, premium or other insurance related charges, as provided under the compulsory worker’s insurance coverage.\nThe persons criminally liable for the above offenses are the principals, accomplices and accessories. In case of juridical persons, the officers having ownership, control, management or direction of their business who are responsible for the commission of the offense and the responsible employees/agents thereof shall be liable.\nIn the filing of cases for illegal recruitment or any of the prohibited acts under this section, the Secretary of Labor and Employment, the POEA Administrator or their duly authorized representatives, or any aggrieved person may initiate the corresponding criminal action with the appropriate office. For this purpose, the affidavits and testimonies of operatives or personnel from the Department of Labor and Employment, POEA and other law enforcement agencies who witnessed the acts constituting the offense shall be sufficient to prosecute the accused.\nIn the prosecution of offenses punishable under this section, the public prosecutors of the Department of Justice shall collaborate with the anti-illegal recruitment branch of the POEA and, in certain cases, allow the POEA lawyers to take the lead in the prosecution. The POEA lawyers who act as prosecutors in such cases shall be entitled to receive additional allowances as may be determined by the POEA Administrator.\nThe filing of an offense punishable under this Act shall be without prejudice to the filing of cases punishable under other existing laws, rules or regulations.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"sec7":{"title":"R.A. No. 8042, Section 7 — Penalties","text":"SEC. 7. Penalties. —\n(a) Any person found guilty of illegal recruitment shall suffer the penalty of imprisonment of not less than twelve (12) years and one (1) day but not more than twenty (20) years and a fine of not less than One million pesos (P1,000,000.00) nor more than Two million pesos (P2,000,000.00).\n(b) The penalty of life imprisonment and a fine of not less than Two million pesos (P2,000,000.00) nor more than Five million pesos (P5,000,000.00) shall be imposed if illegal recruitment constitutes economic sabotage as defined therein.\nProvided, however, That the maximum penalty shall be imposed if the person illegally recruited is less than eighteen (18) years of age or committed by a non-licensee or non-holder of authority.\n(c) Any person found guilty of any of the prohibited acts shall suffer the penalty of imprisonment of not less than six (6) years and one (1) day but not more than twelve (12) years and a fine of not less than Five hundred thousand pesos (P500,000.00) nor more than One million pesos (P1,000,000.00).\nIf the offender is an alien, he or she shall, in addition to the penalties herein prescribed, be deported without further proceedings.\nIn every case, conviction shall cause and carry the automatic revocation of the license or registration of the recruitment/manning agency, lending institutions, training school or medical clinic.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"sec8":{"title":"R.A. No. 8042, Section 8 — Prohibition on Officials and Employees","text":"SEC. 8. Prohibition on Officials and Employees. — It shall be unlawful for any official or employee of the Department of Labor and Employment, the Philippine Overseas Employment Administration (POEA), or the Overseas Workers Welfare Administration (OWWA), or the Department of Foreign Affairs, or other government agencies involved in the implementation of this Act, or their relatives within the fourth civil degree of consanguinity or affinity, to engage, directly or indirectly, in the business of recruiting migrant workers as defined in this Act. The penalties provided in the immediately preceding paragraph shall be imposed upon them.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"sec9":{"title":"R.A. No. 8042, Section 9 — Venue","text":"SEC. 9. Venue. — A criminal action arising from illegal recruitment as defined herein shall be filed with the Regional Trial Court of the province or city where the offense was committed or where the offended party actually resides at the time of the commission of the offense: Provided, That the court where the criminal action is first filed shall acquire jurisdiction to the exclusion of other courts: Provided, however, That the aforestated provisions shall also apply to those criminal actions that have already been filed in court at the time of the effectivity of this Act.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"sec10":{"title":"R.A. No. 8042, Section 10 — Money Claims","text":"SEC. 10. Money Claims. — Notwithstanding any provision of law to the contrary, the Labor Arbiters of the National Labor Relations Commission (NLRC) shall have the original and exclusive jurisdiction to hear and decide, within ninety (90) calendar days after the filing of the complaint, the claims arising out of an employer-employee relationship or by virtue of any law or contract involving Filipino workers for overseas deployment including claims for actual, moral, exemplary and other forms of damage. Consistent with this mandate, the NLRC shall endeavor to update and keep abreast with the developments in the global services industry.\nThe liability of the principal/employer and the recruitment/placement agency for any and all claims under this section shall be joint and several. This provision shall be incorporated in the contract for overseas employment and shall be a condition precedent for its approval. The performance bond to be filed by the recruitment/placement agency, as provided by law, shall be answerable for all money claims or damages that may be awarded to the workers. If the recruitment/placement agency is a juridical being, the corporate officers and directors and partners as the case may be, shall themselves be jointly and solidarily liable with the corporation or partnership for the aforesaid claims and damages.\nSuch liabilities shall continue during the entire period or duration of the employment contract and shall not be affected by any substitution, amendment or modification made locally or in a foreign country of the said contract.\nAny compromise/amicable settlement or voluntary agreement on money claims inclusive of damages under this section shall be paid within thirty (30) days from the approval of the settlement by the appropriate authority.\nIn case of termination of overseas employment without just, valid or authorized cause as defined by law or contract, or any unauthorized deductions from the migrant worker’s salary, the worker shall be entitled to the full reimbursement of his placement fee and the deductions made with interest at twelve percent (12%) per annum, plus his salaries for the unexpired portion of his employment contract or for three (3) months for every year of the unexpired term, whichever is less.\nIn case of a final and executory judgement against a foreign employer/principal, it shall be automatically disqualified, without further proceedings, from participating in the Philippine Overseas Employment Program and from recruiting and hiring Filipino workers until and unless it fully satisfies the judgement award.\nNoncompliance with the mandatory periods for resolutions of case provided under this section shall subject the responsible officials to any or all of the following penalties:\n(a) The salary of any such official who fails to render his decision or resolution within the prescribed period shall be, or caused to be, withheld until the said official complies therewith;\n(b) Suspension for not more than ninety (90) days; or\n(c) Dismissal from the service with disqualification to hold any appointive public office for five (5) years.\nProvided, however, That the penalties herein provided shall be without prejudice to any liability which any such official may have incured under other existing laws or rules and regulations as a consequence of violating the provisions of this paragraph.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"sec11":{"title":"R.A. No. 8042, Section 11 — Mandatory Periods","text":"SEC. 11. Mandatory Periods for Resolution of Illegal Recruitment Cases. — The preliminary investigations of cases under this Act shall be terminated within a period of thirty (30) calendar days from the date of their filing. Where the preliminary investigation is conducted by a prosecution officer and a prima facie case is established, the corresponding information shall be filed in court within twenty-four (24) hours from the termination of the investigation. If the preliminary investigation is conducted by a judge and a prima facie case is found to exist, the corresponding information shall be filed by the proper prosecution officer within forty-eight (48) hours from the date of receipt of the records of the case.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"sec12":{"title":"R.A. No. 8042, Section 12 — Prescriptive Periods","text":"SEC. 12. Prescriptive Periods. — Illegal recruitment cases under this Act shall prescribe in five (5) years: Provided, however, That illegal recruitment cases involving economic sabotage as defined herein shall prescribe in twenty (20) years.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"ra11641":{"title":"R.A. No. 11641, Section 6(c) — DMW Power","text":"SEC. 6. Powers and Functions. — To carry out its mandate, the Department shall:\n(c) Investigate, initiate, sue, pursue, and help prosecute, in cooperation with the Department of Justice (DOJ) and the Inter-Agency Council Against Trafficking (IACAT), illegal recruitment and human trafficking cases as defined under Republic Act No. 8042, as amended by Republic Act No. 10022, otherwise known as the Migrant Workers and Overseas Filipinos Act of 1995, as amended, and as provided under Republic Act No. 9208, as amended by Republic Act No. 10364, otherwise known as the Anti-Trafficking in Persons Act, and other existing laws and other issuances. In the performance of its functions, the Department Secretary and his or her authorized deputy shall have the power: (1) to issue subpoena or subpoena duces tecum to any person for investigation for illegal recruitment or trafficking in persons cases as defined under Republic Act No. 9208, as amended, and other existing laws and other issuances; and hold or cite any person in contempt as may be provided by the implementing rules and regulations; (2) to administer oaths upon cases under investigation; and (3) to have access to all public records and to records of private parties and concerns, in accordance with law;","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"poea76":{"title":"2016 Revised POEA Rules, Rule X, Section 76 — Acts Constituting Illegal Recruitment","text":"SECTION 76. Acts Constituting Illegal Recruitment. — Illegal Recruitment shall mean any act of canvassing, enlisting, contracting, transporting, utilizing, hiring or procuring workers and includes referrals, contract services, promising or advertising for employment abroad, whether for profit or not, when undertaken by a non-licensee or non-holder of authority contemplated under Article 13(f) of Presidential Decree No. 442, as amended, otherwise known as the Labor Code of the Philippines: Provided, that any such non-licensee or non-holder who, in any manner, offers or promises for a fee employment abroad to two or more persons shall be deemed so engaged.\n\nIt shall likewise include the following prohibited acts committed by any person whether or not a licensee or a holder of authority:\n\n(a) To charge or accept directly or indirectly any amount greater than that specified in the schedule of allowable fees prescribed by the Secretary or to make a worker pay the recruiter or its agents or acknowledge any amount greater than that actually loaned or advanced to him;\n\n(b) To furnish or publish any false notice or information or document in relation to recruitment or employment;\n\n(c) To give any false notice, testimony, information or document or commit any act of misrepresentation for the purpose of securing a license or authority under the Labor Code;\n\n(d) To give any false notice, testimony, information or document or commit any act of misrepresentation for the purpose of documenting hired workers with the POEA, which include the act of reprocessing workers through a job order that pertains to non-existent work, work different from the actual overseas work, or work with a different employer whether registered or not with the POEA;\n\n(e) To induce or attempt to induce a worker already employed to quit his/her employment in order to offer him another unless the transfer is designed to liberate a worker from oppressive terms and conditions of employment;\n\n(f) To influence or attempt to influence any person or entity not to employ any worker who has not applied for employment through his/her agency or who has formed, joined or supported, or has contacted or is supported by any union or workers’ organization;\n\n(g) To engage in the recruitment or placement of workers in jobs harmful to public health or morality or to the dignity of the Republic of the Philippines as may be prohibited by law or duly constituted authority;\n\n(h) To obstruct or attempt to obstruct inspection by the Secretary or by his/her duly authorized representative;\n\n(i) To fail to submit reports on the status of employment, placement vacancies, remittance of foreign exchange earnings, separation from jobs, departures and such other matters or information as may be required by the Secretary under penalty of law;\n\n(j) To substitute or alter to the prejudice of the worker, employment contract approved and verified by the DOLE from the time of actual signing thereof by the parties up to and including the period of the expiration of the same without the approval of the DOLE;\n\n(k) For an officer or agent of a recruitment or placement agency to become an officer or member of the Board of any corporation engaged in travel agency or insurance agency or to be engaged directly or indirectly in the management of a travel agency or insurance agency;\n\n(l) To withhold or deny travel documents from applicant workers before departure for monetary or financial considerations, or for any other reasons, other than those authorized under the Labor Code and its implementing rules and regulations;\n\n(m) To fail to actually deploy a contracted worker without valid reason as determined by the DOLE;\n\n(n) To fail to reimburse expenses incurred by the worker in connection with his/her documentation and processing for purposes of deployment, in cases where the deployment does not actually take place without the worker's fault;\n\n(o) To allow a non-Filipino citizen to head or manage a recruitment agency;\n\n(p) To arrange, facilitate or grant a loan to an Overseas Filipino Worker with interest exceeding eight percent (8%) per annum, which will be used for payment of legal and allowable placement fees and make the migrant worker issue, either personally or through a guarantor or accommodation party, postdated checks in relation to the said loan;\n\n(q) To impose a compulsory and exclusive arrangement whereby an Overseas Filipino Worker is required to avail of a loan only from specifically designated institutions, entities or persons;\n\n(r) To refuse to condone or renegotiate a loan incurred by an Overseas Filipino Worker after the latter’s employment contract has been prematurely terminated through no fault of his/her own;\n\n(s) To impose a compulsory and exclusive arrangement whereby an Overseas Filipino Worker is required to undergo health examinations only from specifically designated medical clinics, institutions, entities or persons, except in the case of a worker whose medical examination cost is shouldered by the principal;\n\n(t) To impose a compulsory and exclusive arrangement whereby an Overseas Filipino Worker is required to undergo training, seminar, instruction or schooling of any kind only from specifically designated institutions, entities or persons, except for recommendatory trainings mandated by principals where the latter shoulder the cost of such trainings;\n\n(u) For a suspended recruitment agency to engage in any kind of recruitment activity including the processing of pending workers’ applications; and\n\n(v) For a recruitment agency or a foreign principal/employer to pass on to the Overseas Filipino Worker or deduct from his/her salary the payment of the cost of insurance fees, premium or other insurance related charges, as provided under the compulsory worker’s insurance coverage.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"art13b":{"title":"Labor Code, Article 13(b) — Recruitment and Placement","text":"“Recruitment and placement” refers to any act of canvassing, enlisting, contracting, transporting, utilizing, hiring or procuring workers, and includes referrals, contract services, promising or advertising for employment, locally or abroad, whether for profit or not: Provided, That any person or entity which, in any manner, offers or promises for a fee employment to two or more persons shall be deemed engaged in recruitment and placement.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Law File — supplied study reference; check amendments for current law"},"art299summary":{"title":"Labor Code, Article 299 — Disease as Ground for Termination","text":"Study summary linked to the Yarza report: Disease-related dismissal is not automatic. Analyze the legally required ground, certification by a competent public health authority, and other statutory safeguards under Article 299, together with the case facts. This is not verbatim statutory text.","source":"Yarza checkpoint and Labor report; study summary (not verbatim)"},"contractsummary":{"title":"Overseas Employment — Nondeployment / Contract","text":"Study summary linked to Stolt-Nielsen v. Medequillo: Differentiate perfection of the overseas employment contract, its commencement/effectivity for deployment, and remedies for a breach or nondeployment. Examine the governing POEA terms and specific facts; this is not verbatim statutory text.","source":"Stolt-Nielsen checkpoint and uploaded Labor report; study summary"}},"cases":{"toston":{"name":"Toston v. People","citation":"G.R. No. 232049, March 3, 2021","doctrine":"An employee of a licensed recruitment agency is not automatically criminally liable for illegal recruitment merely because he performed ordinary recruitment-related tasks. The prosecution must prove his conscious and active participation in the unlawful conduct; where he acted within his job description under a validly licensed agency and the charged nondeployment had a valid reason, criminal liability does not attach.","facts":"Mary Ann Soliven responded to an overseas job opportunity associated with Steadfast International Recruitment Corporation. Toston, an employee of Steadfast, conducted a preliminary interview, referred her to the recruitment assistant, and referred her for the required medical examination. Other agency personnel later handled the placement fee, which Mary Ann paid in the amount of about ₱50,000. Toston was not shown to have received the fee. Mary Ann was not deployed to Singapore. The records, however, showed that she had been medically found unfit for overseas deployment and that the prosecution failed to prove Toston knew of the result or participated in any concealment. Steadfast had also asked her to wait for the deployment period, but before that period elapsed she withdrew and pursued a complaint after seeing online accusations against the agency. The RTC and CA convicted Toston, treating his interview and referrals as recruitment and focusing on questions about his registration as an agency employee.","issue":"Whether Toston was guilty beyond reasonable doubt of illegal recruitment and estafa on account of his participation in Mary Ann’s application and nondeployment.","ruling":"No. The Supreme Court acquitted Toston. The evidence showed that he was a documented employee of a licensed recruitment agency and that the acts attributable to him were routine functions within his job: the preliminary interview and referrals. The prosecution did not prove that he actively joined an illegal scheme, received the placement fee, knew of the adverse medical result, or participated in concealing it. Nondeployment under Section 6(l) was also not criminally attributable to him because the worker was medically unfit and withdrew before the agreed waiting period had expired. The estafa charge likewise failed for lack of proof of deceit personally attributable to Toston.","ratio":"The case applies the principle that criminal liability of agency employees requires personal, conscious, and active participation. Registration and licensing regulations are primarily obligations of the agency and its responsible officers; an ordinary employee who reasonably acts under a licensed agency’s supervision is not made a criminal merely by performing assigned tasks. At the same time, Toston does not immunize employees who knowingly collect money, make false representations, or otherwise participate in an illegal scheme.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"},"liwanag":{"name":"People v. Liwanag","citation":"G.R. No. 232245, March 2, 2022","doctrine":"Large-scale illegal recruitment requires recruitment activity, absence of authority, and proof that the offense was committed against at least three persons. Receipts are not indispensable, and all three victims need not personally testify if competent evidence establishes recruitment of three or more persons.","facts":"Mildred Liwanag represented that she could send four complainants to Japan to work as factory workers in a noodle factory, allegedly through her sister who was working there. She collected around ₱40,500 from each applicant for passports, visa processing, plane tickets, and placement expenses, and represented that they would depart on a specified date. The promised employment did not materialize. A POEA certification established that Liwanag had no license or authority to recruit. She attacked the prosecution’s proof on the ground that not every payment was supported by a receipt and that one of the four complainants did not personally testify. The prosecution witnesses, however, positively described the collective recruitment process and the payments, and the barangay blotter contained an admission by Liwanag that she had received amounts and would repay them.","issue":"Whether absence of receipts and the failure of one alleged victim to testify defeated the charge of illegal recruitment in large scale and the corresponding estafa charges.","ruling":"No. The Court affirmed the convictions. Offering and promising the four complainants jobs in Japan and collecting employment-related fees constituted recruitment activity. Lack of authority was established by the POEA certification. The large-scale element was proven because the evidence as a whole showed recruitment of at least three persons. The law does not require three victims to take the witness stand; it requires proof that three or more were recruited. Receipts are likewise not essential when credible testimony and corroborating evidence establish the transaction. Estafa was separately established because the complainants relied on Liwanag’s false representation of capacity to deploy them, parted with money, and suffered loss.","ratio":"The case clarifies the evidentiary rule later contrasted with Dela Piedra and Ortiz-Miyake. The prosecution may prove the third or additional victim through competent evidence other than that victim’s own testimony, but hearsay or testimony without personal knowledge is insufficient. The rule is therefore not “three victims must testify”; it is “three victims must be proved.”","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"},"delaconcepcion":{"name":"People v. Dela Concepcion","citation":"G.R. No. 251876, March 21, 2022","doctrine":"The absence of receipts is not fatal where credible testimony proves recruitment. The “distinct impression” that the accused had the power to deploy workers abroad may be established by the totality of her acts, including collecting document-processing money, presenting supposed job orders, and assuring applicants of overseas work.","facts":"Mary Jane Dela Concepcion, using aliases, dealt with numerous applicants—more than thirty according to the records—who were promised overseas employment, including work in Italy as caregivers, office personnel, and factory workers. Applicants paid varying amounts for medical examinations, authentication and translation of civil documents, Department of Foreign Affairs processing, and other deployment expenses. Dela Concepcion denied being the true recruiter and characterized herself as merely helping applicants process documents. Several complainants had no formal receipts for their payments. The prosecution nevertheless presented detailed testimony describing the job promises, the collection of money, the documentation process, and the failure to deploy or refund. Dela Concepcion also made admissions concerning the collection of particular processing amounts.","issue":"Whether the evidence showed actual recruitment rather than mere document assistance, and whether the lack of receipts created reasonable doubt as to illegal recruitment and estafa.","ruling":"The appeal was denied. The Court held that her acts collectively created the distinct impression that she possessed the power to deploy the complainants abroad. She had no authority to recruit, collected money for documents that were plainly tied to the represented jobs, and dealt with more than three persons. The absence of receipts did not defeat the prosecution because the complainants testified credibly in open court. Her failure to reimburse qualifying processing expenses after nondeployment also fell within the statutory scheme of Section 6. The Court sustained the relevant convictions for illegal recruitment and estafa, subject to the proper penalties for the individual cases.","ratio":"The case is useful for borderline conduct. Passport, visa, or document assistance by itself may not necessarily be recruitment. But when the accused couples such assistance with employment promises, supposed job orders, deployment assurances, and collection of employment-related money, the distinct-impression test is satisfied. The inquiry is functional: what did the accused’s conduct communicate to the worker?","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"},"sison":{"name":"People v. Sison","citation":"G.R. No. 187160, August 9, 2017","doctrine":"Illegal recruitment is committed by a syndicate when at least three persons conspire or confederate in carrying out the unlawful recruitment scheme. It is not essential that every conspirator personally perform every act; it is enough that they acted in concert toward the same objective. Illegal recruitment and estafa may be separately punished.","facts":"Erlinda Sison told Darvy Castuera that she could facilitate his employment in Australia as a fruit picker. To reinforce her representations, she showed pictures of persons she claimed to have successfully helped and introduced a person who gave a testimonial. She asked for a substantial processing amount, eventually negotiated to ₱160,000, part payable before departure and part from future salary. Castuera made a down payment. Sison later traveled with him through Malaysia, where the promised Australian visa was supposedly to be processed. He encountered Sison’s co-accused, Rea Dedales and Leonardo Bacomo, and the group later proceeded to Indonesia for further visa efforts. The Australian job never materialized. Sison argued that she was merely helping with visa processing and was herself a victim of the others.","issue":"Whether Sison’s acts constituted recruitment; whether the participation of Sison, Dedales, and Bacomo established syndicated illegal recruitment; and whether estafa could also be sustained.","ruling":"Yes. Sison’s representations and conduct gave Castuera the impression that she had the ability to send him abroad for work, which induced him to part with money. The coordinated actions of Sison, Dedales, and Bacomo—receiving payments, signing acknowledgments, accompanying recruits, and participating in the supposed visa process—showed a common objective. The Court held that the three acted in concert, so the illegal recruitment was committed by a syndicate and constituted economic sabotage. Estafa was also established because Sison’s fraudulent representations preceded the payment and caused pecuniary damage.","ratio":"The case separates syndicated recruitment from large-scale recruitment. Syndicate focuses on the number and concerted action of recruiters; large scale focuses on the number of victims. Either qualifying circumstance independently makes the offense economic sabotage. The conspiracy rule is practical: division of labor among recruiters does not shield those who knowingly perform different parts of a common scheme.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"},"ocden":{"name":"People v. Ocden","citation":"G.R. No. 173198, June 1, 2011","doctrine":"A person cannot avoid liability by describing herself as a fellow applicant or mere coordinator when her actual conduct—collecting applications and money, accompanying applicants, and acting as the point person for the supposed overseas jobs—constitutes recruitment and placement.","facts":"Dolores Ocden was implicated in a scheme promising factory work in Italy to several applicants in Baguio. A person named Ramos conducted seminars, including at Ocden’s house, and designated Ocden as leader of the applicants. Ocden collected or received application papers and placement money from co-applicants, accompanied them to Manila for medical examinations because she knew the clinic, and served as the local point of contact. She argued that Ramos was the true recruiter, that she herself paid Ramos as an applicant, and that she later joined complaints against him. She claimed that amounts she received from applicants were merely turned over to Ramos. The RTC nonetheless convicted her of illegal recruitment in large scale and several counts of estafa; the CA affirmed with modifications.","issue":"Whether Ocden’s own status as an alleged applicant or intermediary negated her criminal liability for large-scale illegal recruitment and estafa.","ruling":"No. The Supreme Court found no basis to reverse the convictions. Ocden’s objective acts demonstrated recruitment participation: she handled applications, dealt directly with applicants, received substantial placement amounts, and helped move the recruitment process forward. More than three persons were involved, satisfying large scale. The Court also sustained the estafa convictions that were supported by evidence of false promises, payments, and resulting loss.","ratio":"The controlling inquiry is the accused’s conduct, not her self-description. A person may simultaneously be deceived by an upstream recruiter and still commit recruitment acts toward other applicants. Being a “leader,” “coordinator,” “friend,” or “fellow applicant” is not a defense if the statutory acts are actually performed.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"},"delapiedra":{"name":"People v. Dela Piedra","citation":"G.R. No. 121777, January 24, 2001","doctrine":"Large-scale illegal recruitment requires competent proof that at least three persons were recruited. Although the law does not require all three to testify personally, hearsay evidence concerning the third alleged victim is insufficient; the accused also cannot be convicted based on persons not properly alleged in the information.","facts":"Carol Dela Piedra was charged with illegal recruitment in large scale for allegedly promising overseas work to Nancy Araneta, Lourdes Modesto, and Jennelyn Baez. Araneta and Modesto testified credibly about a briefing, employment promises, and fees. The prosecution’s proof concerning Baez was much weaker. Baez did not testify, and a joint affidavit was not properly identified or affirmed by her. The prosecution also pointed to other persons present during recruitment activity, including an enforcement operative, but those persons were not the victims alleged in the information as the basis of the large-scale charge. The trial court convicted Dela Piedra of illegal recruitment in large scale and imposed life imprisonment.","issue":"Whether the evidence sufficiently proved recruitment of three or more persons so as to sustain large-scale illegal recruitment.","ruling":"No. The Court held that only Araneta and Modesto were proven beyond reasonable doubt to have been recruited. The evidence as to Baez was hearsay and lacked adequate probative value. Other persons could not simply be substituted as the third victim when the information did not charge their recruitment; doing so would violate the accused’s right to be informed of the nature and cause of the accusation. The large-scale conviction was therefore reduced to the illegal recruitment offenses actually proven against the two complainants.","ratio":"The case supplies the evidentiary limit to the Liwanag rule. Three victims need not all testify, but there must still be competent evidence of three victims. The right to be informed also matters: the prosecution cannot cure a deficient large-scale allegation by counting unrelated persons outside the charge.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"},"ortizmiyake":{"name":"People v. Ortiz-Miyake","citation":"G.R. Nos. 115338-39, September 16, 1997","doctrine":"A conviction for large-scale illegal recruitment must be based on evidence in the case proving recruitment of at least three persons. Testimony from relatives without personal knowledge and factual findings lifted from a different criminal case cannot substitute for competent proof. The accused may nevertheless be convicted of the lesser offense of simple illegal recruitment.","facts":"Lanie Ortiz-Miyake was charged with large-scale illegal recruitment involving Rosamar del Rosario, Elenita Marasigan, and Imelda Generillo, together with a separate estafa charge concerning Marasigan. Marasigan personally testified that Ortiz-Miyake represented that she could send her to Taiwan as a factory worker and received a total of about ₱23,000 in installments. The other two complainants were abroad and did not testify. Their relatives attempted to describe the alleged transactions, but they had not personally witnessed the negotiations or payments. The trial court also relied on findings from a prior Parañaque decision involving related matters rather than independent evidence presented in the case before it.","issue":"Whether the prosecution sufficiently proved illegal recruitment in large scale against all three named complainants and whether the estafa conviction involving Marasigan could stand.","ruling":"The Court reduced the illegal-recruitment conviction to simple illegal recruitment. It found competent proof regarding Marasigan but not the other two complainants. Relatives who were not privy to the transactions lacked personal knowledge, and the decision of another court could not itself serve as evidence of facts in the present criminal case. The estafa conviction as to Marasigan was affirmed because her testimony proved the false representation, reliance, payment, and damage.","ratio":"Every conviction must rest on evidence properly presented and tested in the particular case. Ortiz-Miyake therefore reinforces both the evidentiary rule for the “three victims” requirement and the confrontation/personal-knowledge principles that constrain criminal adjudication.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"},"sunace":{"name":"Sunace Int’l Management Services, Inc. v. NLRC","citation":"G.R. No. 161757, January 25, 2006","doctrine":"The theory of imputed knowledge generally attributes the agent’s knowledge to the principal, not the principal’s knowledge to the agent. A recruitment agency is not automatically liable for obligations arising from a new post-expiration employment extension when substantial evidence does not show that the agency knew of, consented to, or was privy to that extension.","facts":"Sunace deployed Divina Montehermozo to Taiwan as a domestic helper under a 12-month contract. When the original contract expired, Divina continued working directly for the Taiwanese employer for about two additional years. After her return to the Philippines, she filed monetary claims relating in part to the extended period and sought to hold Sunace liable. The lower tribunals reasoned that because the foreign principal extended the employment and there had been communications between Sunace and a Taiwanese broker, Sunace should be deemed to know of and be bound by the extension. The communication relied upon, however, primarily concerned withheld savings and was sent after the complaint was already filed; it did not establish that Sunace had previously known of or consented to the two-year extension.","issue":"Whether the foreign employer’s knowledge of and participation in the employment extension could automatically be imputed to Sunace so as to make the Philippine agency solidarily liable for claims arising from the extended period.","ruling":"No. The Court held that the theory of imputed knowledge had been misapplied. Knowledge of the agent may be imputed to the principal, but knowledge of the principal is not automatically imputed backward to the agent. There was no substantial proof that Sunace knew of or agreed to be bound by the new extension. Because the foreign principal had directly dealt with Divina after the original contract expired, the Court also discussed the Civil Code rule on implied revocation of agency when the principal directly manages the entrusted business.","ratio":"Sunace is not a blanket rejection of solidary liability. Its significance is factual and conceptual: statutory agency liability does not eliminate the need to establish that the claim actually arises from the contract or relationship for which the agency is responsible. A genuinely new, direct arrangement entered after expiration, without the agency’s knowledge or participation, may fall outside that liability.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"},"becmen":{"name":"Becmen Service Exporter and Promotion, Inc. v. Cuaresma","citation":"G.R. Nos. 182978-79, April 7, 2009","doctrine":"Recruitment agencies and foreign principals have a continuing protective responsibility toward deployed OFWs. Their solidary liability is intended to assure immediate and sufficient payment of valid claims, and wanton or oppressive mishandling of a distressed worker’s case may justify moral and exemplary damages.","facts":"Jasmin Cuaresma was deployed to Saudi Arabia as an assistant nurse under a three-year overseas employment contract. More than a year later she was found dead in her dormitory. Initial Saudi reports referred to apparent poisoning, while a later Philippine examination raised serious questions and indicated injuries inconsistent with a simple, unexplained suicide narrative. Jasmin’s parents sought accountability and assistance from the foreign principal and the Philippine agencies involved in her deployment. The record showed indifference, failure to meaningfully assist in clarifying the circumstances of her death, and an insistence on a version of events that minimized liability despite contradictory evidence. The litigation involved Becmen, White Falcon, the foreign employer, and responsible corporate officers.","issue":"Whether the recruitment agencies and foreign employer could be held jointly and solidarily liable, including for moral and exemplary damages arising from their handling of Jasmin’s death and the family’s claims.","ruling":"Yes. The Court emphasized the public policy of full protection to labor, including overseas workers. Private recruitment agencies are jointly and severally liable with foreign employers for violations arising from the recruitment agreement and employment contract. The agencies’ callous and oppressive treatment of Jasmin’s case and their failure to extend appropriate assistance supported moral and exemplary damages. One agency’s assumption of liability did not automatically release the other from its solidary obligation; rights of reimbursement among liable parties are separate from the worker’s right to collect.","ratio":"The agency is not simply a pre-departure broker. Licensing carries a continuing covenant with the State to protect workers and ensure proper contract implementation. Becmen is therefore frequently used, including in Yarza, to explain why agency responsibility may continue during the life of the overseas employment relationship and why distress cases implicate public policy.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"},"datuman":{"name":"Datuman v. First Cosmopolitan Manpower and Promotion Services, Inc.","citation":"G.R. No. 156029, November 14, 2008","doctrine":"A local recruitment agency remains jointly and solidarily liable for breaches of the POEA-approved employment contract. A prejudicial “substitute” contract imposed abroad cannot be used to cut off the agency’s liability; the substitution may itself constitute a continuing breach of the original approved contract.","facts":"Santosa Datuman was recruited to Bahrain under a POEA-approved contract as a saleslady with a salary of US$370 per month for one year. Upon arrival, the foreign employer took her passport and required her to work instead as a domestic helper for about BD40, roughly US$100, far below the approved wage. She was later compelled to sign another contract transferring her to another employer as a housemaid for a longer period and under inferior conditions. She repeatedly sought release and return of her passport and eventually worked for extended periods under coercive circumstances, including periods of nonpayment, before obtaining assistance to return to the Philippines. The local agency argued that its responsibility should be limited to the original contract period and that later arrangements were outside its control.","issue":"Whether the substituted contracts and continuation of employment abroad terminated or limited the Philippine agency’s solidary liability for Datuman’s monetary claims.","ruling":"No. The Court held the agency jointly and solidarily liable with the foreign principal. The compelled substitute contracts did not supersede the original POEA-approved contract to Datuman’s prejudice. The substitution and continuation of work under inferior terms were continuing breaches of the approved employment agreement. Accepting the agency’s theory would create an easy device for local recruiters and foreign employers to evade mandatory worker protections immediately after deployment.","ratio":"The original POEA-approved contract supplies the protective minimum terms. A side or substitute agreement that reduces benefits below approved standards and is imposed to the worker’s prejudice is void as against law and public policy. Datuman therefore shows why Section 10 liability continues despite substitution, amendment, or modification when those devices would otherwise defeat OFW protection.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"},"ipams":{"name":"Industrial Personnel & Management Services, Inc. v. De Vera","citation":"G.R. No. 205703, March 7, 2016","doctrine":"Philippine labor law is the general rule for overseas employment contracts. A foreign law may govern only if it is expressly stipulated in the contract, properly proven under Philippine evidence rules, not contrary to Philippine law/morals/good customs/public order/public policy, and the overseas employment contract was processed through the POEA. Failure of any requisite results in application of Philippine law.","facts":"Alberto Arriola, a Filipino physician, was hired through IPAMS by SNC-Lavalin for the Ambatovy Project in Madagascar as a Safety Officer. The employment offer contemplated a fixed period and stated a Canadian-dollar hourly rate. The contract was signed in the Philippines and processed through the POEA. Before the end of the term, the foreign employer pre-terminated Arriola, citing diminishing workload and lack of alternative assignments. It paid an amount calculated under Canadian labor law and argued that the Canadian Employment Standards Act governed through an expatriate policy. Arriola insisted that the supposed foreign-law choice was not clearly contained in the overseas employment contract, that the foreign law had not been properly incorporated, and that its dismissal provisions conflicted with Philippine security-of-tenure and due-process norms.","issue":"When may a foreign law govern an OFW employment contract, and did Canadian law validly govern Arriola’s premature termination?","ruling":"The Court ruled that Philippine law governed. It synthesized four cumulative requisites for application of foreign law: (1) express stipulation of the specific foreign law in the overseas employment contract; (2) proper proof of the foreign law; (3) consistency with Philippine law, morals, good customs, public order, and public policy; and (4) POEA processing of the contract. The employer failed the test. No specific Canadian law was clearly stipulated on the face of Arriola’s contract, and the provisions invoked were inconsistent with Philippine security of tenure and due process because they allowed termination without the substantive and procedural protections required by Philippine law.","ratio":"The case combines conflict-of-laws principles with constitutional labor protection. If no foreign law is expressly chosen, lex loci contractus/celebrationis points strongly toward Philippine law when the contract is executed here. If foreign law is not proven, processual presumption treats it as the same as Philippine law. Even a validly chosen and proven foreign law cannot override mandatory Philippine public policy protecting workers.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"},"yarza":{"name":"SRL International Manpower Agency v. Yarza, Jr.","citation":"G.R. No. 207828, February 14, 2022","doctrine":"Medical unfitness does not by itself justify termination. Dismissal on the ground of disease requires the legal requisites for disease termination, including the required certification from a competent public health authority, together with observance of procedural due process. An illegally dismissed OFW may recover salaries for the full unexpired portion of the fixed-term contract; the local recruitment agency may be solidarily liable with the foreign principal when its participation is established.","facts":"Pedro Yarza was hired as a project manager in the UAE under a two-year employment arrangement. After working for roughly five months, he returned to the Philippines in connection with employment-visa processing. He underwent another medical examination and was declared medically unfit because of diabetes. The foreign employer thereafter terminated his employment. The record also showed the local agency’s participation in Yarza’s recruitment and deployment despite the use of a visit visa.","issue":"Whether Yarza was validly terminated on medical grounds; whether the local agency was solidarily liable; and what monetary relief followed from the illegal dismissal.","ruling":"The Court held that Yarza was illegally dismissed. The employer failed to establish the requisites for a valid disease-based termination, including the required certification from a competent public health authority that the disease could not be cured within six months even with proper treatment. The private clinic findings were insufficient. Procedural due process was likewise not observed. The Court held the local agency and foreign principal solidarily liable and awarded AED152,000, representing the remaining 19 months of basic salary at AED8,000 per month, plus moral and exemplary damages, attorney’s fees, and costs.","ratio":"OFWs remain entitled to security of tenure. A medical finding is not a shortcut around the statutory safeguards for disease termination. The case also applies the rule, reinforced by Sameer Overseas, that an illegally dismissed migrant worker is not limited by the unconstitutional three-month salary cap and may recover salaries for the actual unexpired portion of the contract.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"},"stolt":{"name":"Stolt-Nielsen Transportation Group, Inc. v. Medequillo, Jr.","citation":"G.R. No. 177498, January 18, 2012","doctrine":"Perfection of an overseas employment contract is distinct from commencement of the employer-employee relationship. A later contract may novate an earlier agreement, and a perfected replacement contract creates binding obligations even before actual deployment. Unjustified nondeployment may therefore constitute breach and support actual damages.","facts":"Sulpecio Medequillo first served as Third Assistant Engineer aboard MV Stolt Aspiration under a nine-month contract but was repatriated after about three months. The parties then executed a second POEA-approved contract assigning him to MV Stolt Pride. He was never deployed under that second contract. The employer argued that employment under the standard seafarer contract begins only upon actual departure from the point of hire and that nondeployment was primarily an administrative matter.","issue":"Whether the second contract novated the first; whether lack of actual deployment prevented enforceable contractual liability; and whether administrative sanctions for nondeployment excluded a damages claim.","ruling":"The Court treated the second contract as replacing the first and held that the claim under the first contract was no longer the controlling basis. Although the employer-employee relationship under the second contract had not yet commenced because Medequillo never departed, the second agreement had already been perfected by consent and therefore created reciprocal obligations. The unjustified failure to deploy breached that perfected contract. Administrative sanctions did not bar a separate damages claim. Medequillo was awarded actual damages equivalent to nine months’ salary under the second contract.","ratio":"Commencement determines when actual labor service begins; perfection determines when contractual obligations become binding. Thus, 'no deployment' does not necessarily mean 'no enforceable rights.' A party who unjustifiably refuses to perform a perfected overseas employment contract may incur contractual liability even before actual work starts.","source":"Uploaded Labor Report, Case File — original wording","source_type":"original_report","source_label":"Original Labor report Case File"}}},"succession":{"laws":{"art886":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 886 — Legitime","text":"Article 886. Legitime is that part of the testator’s property which he cannot dispose of because the law has reserved it for certain heirs who are, therefore, called compulsory heirs. (806)","source":"Succession block notes, p. 1; provision excerpt"},"art887":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 887 — Compulsory Heirs","text":"Article 887 identifies legitimate children and descendants; in default, legitimate parents and ascendants; the widow or widower; and the classes of illegitimate children identified in the Civil Code. The Family Code later abolished the prior distinctions among illegitimate children.\n\nStudy note: The reviewer classifies the heirs as primary, secondary, and concurring, with exclusion and representation rules. This is a condensed study rendering, not the complete original provision.","source":"Succession block notes, pp. 1–2; condensed study rendering"},"art888":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 888 — Legitimate Children","text":"Article 888. The legitime of legitimate children and descendants consists of one-half of the hereditary estate of the father and of the mother.\n\nThe latter may freely dispose of the remaining half, subject to the rights of illegitimate children and of the surviving spouse as hereinafter provided. (808a)","source":"Succession block notes, p. 2; provision excerpt"},"art892":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 892 — Children + Surviving Spouse","text":"Article 892. If only one legitimate child or descendant of the deceased survives, the widow or widower shall be entitled to one-fourth of the hereditary estate. In case of a legal separation, the surviving spouse may inherit if it was the deceased who had given cause for the same.\n\nIf there are two or more legitimate children or descendants, the surviving spouse shall be entitled to a portion equal to the legitime of each of the legitimate children or descendants.\n\nIn both cases, the legitime of the surviving spouse shall be taken from the portion that can be freely disposed of by the testator. (834a)","source":"Succession block notes, p. 3; provision excerpt"},"art904":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 904 — Protection of Legitime","text":"Article 904. The testator cannot deprive his compulsory heirs of their legitime, except in cases expressly specified by law.\n\nNeither can he impose upon the same any burden, encumbrance, condition, or substitution of any kind whatsoever. (813a)","source":"Succession block notes, p. 6; provision excerpt"},"art907":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 907 — Inofficious Dispositions","text":"Article 907. Testamentary dispositions that impair or diminish the legitime of the compulsory heirs shall be reduced on petition of the same, insofar as they may be inofficious or excessive. (817)","source":"Succession block notes, p. 6; provision excerpt"},"art908":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 908 — Computing Legitime","text":"Article 908. To determine the legitime, the value of the property left at the death of the testator shall be considered, deducting all debts and charges, which shall not include those imposed in the will.\n\nTo the net value of the hereditary estate, shall be added the value of all donations by the testator that are subject to collation, at the time he made them. (818a)","source":"Succession block notes, p. 6; provision excerpt"},"art909":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 909 — Donations to Children","text":"Article 909. Donations given to children shall be charged to their legitime.\n\nDonations made to strangers shall be charged to that part of the estate of which the testator could have disposed by his last will.\n\nInsofar as they may be inofficious or may exceed the disposable portion, they shall be reduced according to the rules established by this Code. (819a)","source":"Succession block notes, p. 7; provision excerpt"},"art910":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 910 — Donations to Illegitimate Children","text":"Article 910. Donations which an illegitimate child may have received during the lifetime of his father or mother, shall be charged to his legitime.\n\nShould they exceed the portion that can be freely disposed of, they shall be reduced in the manner prescribed by this Code. (847a)","source":"Succession block notes, p. 7; provision excerpt"},"art911":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 911 — Reduction Order","text":"Study summary: Respect lifetime donations as long as the legitime can be covered. Reduce ordinary devises and legacies pro rata when necessary, subject to a priority the testator validly directed. The reviewer explains that donations may subsequently be reduced in inverse date order, and that special election rules apply to a usufruct or life annuity. Read the complete Article 911 for each qualification.","source":"Succession block notes, p. 7; study summary (not verbatim)"},"art912":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 912 — Indivisible Devise","text":"Study summary: Where a devise of real property must be reduced but cannot conveniently be divided, the property generally goes to the devisee if the required reduction is less than one-half of its value; if the reduction reaches one-half or more, it goes to the compulsory heirs. Appropriate cash reimbursement is required. Special rules apply when the devisee is also a compulsory heir.","source":"Succession block notes, p. 7; study summary (not verbatim)"},"art913":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 913 — Public Auction","text":"Article 913. If the heirs or devisees do not choose to avail themselves of the right granted by the preceding article, any heir or devisee who did not have such right may exercise it; should the latter not make use of it, the property shall be sold at public auction at the instance of any one of the interested parties. (822)","source":"Succession block notes, p. 7; provision excerpt"},"art915":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 915 — Disinheritance","text":"Article 915. A compulsory heir may, in consequence of disinheritance, be deprived of his legitime, for causes expressly stated by law. (848a)","source":"Succession block notes, p. 12; provision excerpt"},"art916":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 916 — Valid Will","text":"Article 916. Disinheritance can be effected only through a will wherein the legal cause therefor shall be specified. (849)","source":"Succession block notes, p. 12; provision excerpt"},"art917":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 917 — Proof of Cause","text":"Study summary: If the disinherited heir denies the truth of the cause of disinheritance, the heirs of the testator bear the burden of proving that cause. Refer to the full statutory text in the reviewer.","source":"Succession block notes, pp. 12–13; study summary (not verbatim)"},"art918":{"title":"Civil Code, Article 918 — Defective Disinheritance","text":"Study summary: If the legal cause is not specified, is unproved when challenged, or is not one of the grounds recognized by law, the disinheritance is ineffective to the extent prescribed by Article 918, including protection of the heir’s legitime. Consult the full article and reviewer for the effect on institutions, legacies and devises.","source":"Succession block notes, p. 13; study summary (not verbatim)"},"art919":{"title":"Civil Code, Articles 919–921 — Enumerated Causes","text":"Study summary: The valid grounds for disinheriting descendants, ascendants, and a surviving spouse are specified separately in Articles 919, 920, and 921. The lists are exclusive. This file is a pointer to the assigned articles rather than a full reproduction of those lists.","source":"Succession block notes, pp. 13–16; study summary (not verbatim)"}},"cases":{"rosales":{"name":"Rosales v. Rosales","citation":"G.R. No. L-40789 (1987)","doctrine":"The surviving spouse mentioned in Article 887 is a compulsory heir only with respect to the estate of the deceased spouse. A surviving daughter-in-law is not a compulsory or intestate heir of her parent-in-law and therefore has no legitime in the latter’s estate. Representation belongs to qualified descendants called by law through blood relationship.","facts":"Petra Rosales died intestate after her son Carterio had already died. Carterio was survived by his widow, Irenea, and their son, Macikequerox. The trial court recognized Macikequerox as representing Carterio but excluded Irenea from Petra’s heirs.","issue":"W/N Irenea, as the widow of Petra’s predeceased son, was a compulsory heir entitled to a share in Petra’s estate - NO","ruling":"Article 887 makes a widow or widower a compulsory heir of the deceased spouse, not of a parent-in-law. Irenea had no blood relationship with Petra and could inherit neither in her own right nor by representation. Carterio’s expectancy was extinguished when he predeceased Petra. Macikequerox inherited directly from Petra by representing Carterio.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 7–8; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"baritua":{"name":"Baritua v. CA","citation":"G.R. No. 82233 (1990)","doctrine":"When a person dies leaving a surviving spouse and legitimate child, the parents are not compulsory heirs and cannot claim indemnity payable to the estate. Payment made in good faith to the proper heirs extinguishes the debtor’s obligation.","facts":"Bienvenido Nacario died in a bus-tricycle accident, leaving a widow, Alicia, and a son. The bus owner and driver settled the resulting obligation with Alicia, who received ₱18,500 and executed a release of claims. Later, Bienvenido’s parents sued the petitioners, claiming reimbursement for the damaged tricycle and funeral expenses. The RTC dismissed the complaint, ruling that payment to Bienvenido’s widow and son, his successors-in-interest, extinguished the petitioners’ obligation. The CA reversed, holding that Alicia’s release could not bind the parents because they sued in their own capacity, and awarded them reimbursement.","issue":"W/N the petitioners remained liable to the deceased’s parents despite having already settled with the widow and child of the deceased. - NO","ruling":"The SC reversed the CA and reinstated the RTC, holding that payment to Alicia was valid under Articles 1231 and 1240 because she and her son were Bienvenido’s successors-in-interest. Since Bienvenido left a legitimate child, his parents were not his successors-in-interest. Their claims for the tricycle and funeral expenses were instead money claims against Bienvenido’s estate, not claims against the petitioners.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 8; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","figures":[{"src":"assets/figures/baritua-family-tree.png","caption":"Original relationship/estate diagram — Succession Block Notes, p. 8","alt":"Family relationship or estate diagram included in the reviewer for Baritua v. CA"}],"source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"vizconde":{"name":"Vizconde v. CA","citation":"G.R. No. 118449 (1998)","doctrine":"The purpose of collation is to establish the amount of the net estate so that the legitime of each compulsory heir can be properly calculated and protected against impairment.","facts":"Estrellita acquired a parcel of land in Valenzuela from her father Rafael via an alleged purchase. Estrellita and her two (2) daughters were killed in an incident which left her estate to be divided among Lauro Vizconde (widower/petitioner) and her parents. Rafael (Estrellita’s father) also died the following year. Ramon Nicolas (Estrellita’s brother) sought to include Lauro in Rafael’s intestate estate proceedings for purposes of collating the proceeds from the Valenzuela property which was allegedly a donation.","issue":"WON the inclusion of Lauro Vizconde (son-in-law) in the intestate proceedings for purposes of collation is proper? - NO","ruling":"Under Article 1061, collation is required strictly and exclusively of compulsory heirs succeeding with other compulsory heirs. Because Lauro is not a compulsory heir of Rafael, he has no interest in Rafael's estate and cannot be compelled to collate.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 8–9; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","figures":[{"src":"assets/figures/vizconde-family-tree.png","caption":"Original relationship/estate diagram — Succession Block Notes, p. 8","alt":"Family relationship or estate diagram included in the reviewer for Vizconde v. CA"}],"source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"lim":{"name":"In re Petition for Adoption of Michelle P. Lim and Michael Jude P. Lim","citation":"G.R. Nos. 168992-93 (2009)","doctrine":"An adoptee is considered a legitimate child of the adopter with all the rights of a legitimate child, including the right to be entitled to the legitime and other successional rights. Conversely, the adoptive parents shall, with respect to the adopted child, enjoy all the benefits to which biological parents are entitled such as successional rights.","facts":"Petitioner Monina P. Lim and her late husband, Primo Lim, registered two entrusted minor children as their own to make it appear that they were the children's parents. After Primo died, Monina remarried an American citizen and subsequently filed separate petitions by herself to legally adopt the now-emancipated children to avail of the amnesty under Republic Act No. 8552 for simulated births.","issue":"WON petitioner, who has remarried, can singly adopt – NO","ruling":"The use of the word \"shall\" in Section 7, Article III of RA 8552 means that joint adoption by the husband and the wife is mandatory. While petitioner Monina argued that joint parental authority is no longer necessary since the children are already emancipated, the Court ruled that parental authority is merely just one of the effects of legal adoption. The adoptee is considered a legitimate child of the adopter, entitling them to the legitime and other successional rights.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 8–9; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","figures":[{"src":"assets/figures/lim-adoption-family-tree.png","caption":"Original relationship/estate diagram — Succession Block Notes, p. 8","alt":"Family relationship or estate diagram included in the reviewer for In re Petition for Adoption of Michelle P. Lim and Michael Jude P. Lim"}],"source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"bartolome":{"name":"Bartolome v. SSS","citation":"G.R. No. 19253 (2014), as stated in reviewer","doctrine":"Adoption does not completely extinguish the legal relationship between an adoptee and his biological parents. Biological parents may retain rights of succession to the estate of their adopted child; under the applicable succession laws cited by the Court, blood relationship continues to have legal consequences notwithstanding adoption.","facts":"John Colcol was legally adopted by his great-grandfather, Cornelio Colcol, when he was a child. Cornelio later died while John was still a minor. John eventually became an electrician aboard a vessel and died in a work-related accident. His biological mother, Bernardina Bartolome, sought death benefits, but the SSS and ECC denied her claim on the theory that adoption had divested her of her status as John's parent.","issue":"W/N the legal adoption of John completely extinguished the legal relationship between him and his biological mother such that she could no longer be recognized as his dependent parent - NO","ruling":"The Court held that adoption did not completely eliminate the legal ties between John and his biological mother. It emphasized that biological parents can still inherit from an adopted child under the succession provisions applicable to the case. Since Cornelio died while John was still a minor, parental authority reverted to Bernardina, and the circumstances also established her dependency on John. She was therefore entitled to benefits as his secondary beneficiary. The Court held that adoption did not completely eliminate the legal ties between John and his biological mother. It emphasized that biological parents can still inherit from an adopted child under the succession provisions applicable to the case. Since Cornelio died while John was still a minor, parental authority reverted to Bernardina, and the circumstances also established her dependency on John. She was therefore entitled to benefits as his secondary beneficiary. The SC cited the following provisions: Civil Code, Article 984: “In case of the death of an adopted child, leaving no children or descendants, his parents and relatives by consanguinity and not by adoption, shall be his legal heirs.” At the time of death of the adoptive parent, this was the law in effect. Family Code, Article 190(2): “When the parents, legitimate or illegitimate, or legitimate ascendants of the adopted concur with the adopter, one-half of the estate goes to the parents or ascendants and the other half to the adopter.” At the time of John's death, this law was in effect.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 9–10; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"francisco":{"name":"Francisco v. Francisco-Alfonso","citation":"G.R. No. 138774 (2001)","doctrine":"A compulsory heir cannot be deprived of his or her legitime except through valid disinheritance prescribed by law. A simulated transfer intended to defeat a compulsory heir’s legitime is void.","facts":"Gregorio Francisco allegedly sold his only two parcels of land to his illegitimate daughters, Regina and Zenaida. His sole legitimate daughter, Aida, challenged the sale. The Court found that no genuine consideration was paid and that the sale was intended to prevent Aida from claiming her legitime.","issue":"Whether a legitimate child and compulsory heir may be deprived of her legitime through a simulated sale of the decedent’s property to his illegitimate children. - NO","ruling":"The Supreme Court held that the sale was simulated and void for lack of consideration. Even assuming it was valid, it could not defeat Aida’s legitime. As the only legitimate child, she was entitled under Article 888 of the Civil Code to her reserved share, subject to the proper settlement of Gregorio’s estate.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 9–10; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"marcos":{"name":"Republic v. Marcos-Manotoc","citation":"G.R. No. 171701 (2012)","doctrine":"In an action for the recovery of a decedent's property that survives death, the compulsory heirs are indispensable parties who must be impleaded to protect the rights now vested in them, and they may not be dropped from the suit merely because their own complicity in the decedent's wrongdoing was not established, since their liability as heirs arises from their status, not from personal fault.","facts":"The Republic, through the PCGG, filed Civil Case No. 0002 before the Sandiganbayan to recover about P200 billion in alleged ill-gotten wealth of the Marcoses, impleading Imelda, the Marcos children, Gregorio Araneta III, the Yeungs (owners of Glorious Sun), and later the Pantranco employees' union as intervenor. After the Republic presented its evidence, the Sandiganbayan granted the demurrers to evidence of the children, Araneta, the Yeungs, and the union, and denied only Imelda's.","issue":"Whether the Marcos siblings, who were found not to have conspired in accumulating the alleged ill-gotten wealth, may still be maintained as defendants in Civil Case No. 0002 as compulsory heirs of Ferdinand E. Marcos - YES","ruling":"The Supreme Court held that the action survives death and the estate must be represented, so the heirs are indispensable parties. As co-owners of the undivided estate, they may possess or control properties that turn out to be ill-gotten, and their lack of participation in any illegal act does not change the character of those properties as belonging to the State. They can be dropped only if they waive in favor of the State their right to defend the estate.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 9; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","figures":[{"src":"assets/figures/marcos-heirs-family-tree.png","caption":"Original relationship/estate diagram — Succession Block Notes, p. 9","alt":"Family relationship or estate diagram included in the reviewer for Republic v. Marcos-Manotoc"}],"source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"rivera":{"name":"Rivera v. Villanueva","citation":"G.R. No. 197310 (2021)","doctrine":"An extrajudicial settlement is not binding upon an heir who did not participate in it or have notice of it. Succession transmits the decedent’s property, rights, and obligations, to the extent of the inheritance, upon death; thus, the right to income accounting begins at death. Because the decedent died before the effectivity of the Family Code, the Civil Code governed the vested inheritance rights, and each acknowledged illegitimate child received four-fifths of the legitime of an acknowledged natural child.","facts":"After Donato Pacheco, Sr. died intestate in 1956, his two legitimate children executed an extrajudicial partition claiming sole ownership of the estate without notifying his four illegitimate children. The excluded illegitimate children subsequently filed an action for partition, title cancellation, and an accounting of estate properties and income. The legitimate heirs argued that the lawsuit was barred by prescription, res judicata, and estoppel, while also disputing property inclusions and legitime computations. In response, the illegitimate children maintained that the original partition was non-binding due to their lack of notice and participation, and that their inheritance and accounting rights had not prescribed.","issue":"W/N each respondent’s legitime should be one-half of the legitime of a legitimate child under the Family Code or four-fifths of the legitime of an acknowledged natural child under Article 895 of the Civil Code. - THE CIVIL CODE APPLIES (⅘ of legitime of acknowledged natural child)","ruling":"The Civil Code applies. Each acknowledged illegitimate child’s legitime is four-fifths of the legitime of an acknowledged natural child. Donato Sr. died on August 21, 1956, during the Civil Code’s effectivity, and respondents’ inheritance rights vested at death. Article 255 of the Family Code preserves vested or acquired rights that would be impaired by retroactive application. Although the CA had applied Article 895 of the Civil Code, the lower courts incorrectly treated respondents as acknowledged natural children. They were acknowledged illegitimate children, not natural children, because their parents were not shown to have been free from an impediment to marry at conception. Under the second paragraph of Article 895, their legitime is therefore four-fifths of the legitime of an acknowledged natural child; Article 888 supplies the relevant half-of-the-hereditary-estate measure for legitimate descendants.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 9–10; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"bellis":{"name":"Bellis v. Bellis","citation":"G.R. No. L-23678 (1967)","doctrine":"The national law of the decedent governs the amount of successional rights and the intrinsic validity of testamentary provisions. Philippine law on legitime (compulsory succession) does not apply to the estate of a foreign national whose national law does not provide for forced heirs or legitimes.","facts":"Amos Bellis was a citizen of Texas and the United States. By his first wife, whom he had divorced, he had five legitimate children (Edward Bellis, et al.), while by his second wife, who survived him, he had three legitimate children. He also had three illegitimate children in the Philippines (Maria Cristina Bellis, et al.). Before his death, Bellis executed a will in the Philippines disposing of his Philippine properties. He had also executed another will covering his properties in the United States, but in both wills, he gave nothing to his illegitimate children. Bellis died in 1958 as a resident and domiciliary of Texas, USA. The executor thereafter sought the probate of the Philippine will. Maria Cristina and her co-illegitimate children opposed the will, arguing that they had been deprived of their legitimes, to which they would be entitled if Philippine law were applied. They invoked Paragraph 3, Article 17 of the New Civil Code, which provides that Philippine laws on property relations shall not be rendered ineffective by foreign laws, judgments, or agreements.","issue":"W/N Philippine law on legitimes or Texas law (the decedent's national law) should govern the successional rights of the illegitimate children? - TEXAS LAW","ruling":"The Supreme Court held that Maria Cristina Bellis and her co-illegitimate children were not entitled to any legitime. Since Texas law was the national law of the deceased and the applicable law, and Texas law does not recognize compulsory legitimes, the children could not claim legitimes under Philippine law. The Court rejected their reliance on Paragraph 3, Article 17 of the New Civil Code, clarifying that this provision is not an exception to the nationality principle under Paragraph 2, Article 16. Rather, the nationality principle governs, as further confirmed by Article 1039, which expressly provides that the capacity to succeed is governed by the law of the nation of the decedent. Thus, the intent of the lawmakers was clear: the succession rights and capacity of the heirs are governed by the national law of the decedent.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 10–11; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","figures":[{"src":"assets/figures/bellis-family-tree.png","caption":"Original relationship/estate diagram — Succession Block Notes, p. 10","alt":"Family relationship or estate diagram included in the reviewer for Bellis v. Bellis"}],"source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"maloles":{"name":"Maloles II v. Philips","citation":"G.R. Nos. 129505 and 133359 (2000)","doctrine":"Compulsory succession protects the legitime of compulsory heirs. A nephew is not a compulsory heir under Article 887; therefore, being the nearest next of kin does not give him a legitime. Under Article 842, a person with no compulsory heirs may freely dispose of his entire estate by will.","facts":"Dr. Arturo de Santos executed a will naming the Arturo de Santos Foundation, Inc. as his sole legatee and devisee and Pacita de los Reyes Phillips as executor. The will was probated during his lifetime, and Dr. de Santos died shortly thereafter. Maloles, claiming to be Dr. de Santos’ sole full-blooded nephew and nearest of kin, sought to intervene in the estate proceedings and claimed to be a creditor. Phillips, meanwhile, sought letters testamentary and was appointed special administrator in a separate proceeding.","issue":"W/N Maloles, as the testator’s nephew and nearest next of kin, is a compulsory heir entitled to a legitime - NO","ruling":"Maloles was not a compulsory heir because nephews are not among those enumerated in Article 887. Thus, he had no legitime that could be impaired or preterited. Since Dr. de Santos had no compulsory heirs, he was free under Article 842 to leave his entire estate to the Foundation. Maloles’ status as nearest next of kin would matter only in intestate succession, which did not apply because there was a valid will disposing of the estate.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 10–11; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"buenaventura":{"name":"Spouses Buenaventura v. CA","citation":"G.R. No. 126376 (2003)","doctrine":"[Children’s] right to their parents' properties is merely inchoate and vests only upon their parents' death; The [sale of property] does not affect the value of [the estate], while [the sale of property] reduced the estate, cash of equivalent value replaced the [property] taken from the estate.","facts":"Petitioners Consolacion, Nora, Emma, and Natividad Joaquin filed a complaint to declare null and void several deeds of absolute sale executed by their living parents, Leonardo Joaquin and Feliciana Landrito, conveying various parcels of land to their sibling-respondents, alleging that the sales were simulated and part of a conspiracy to unjustly deprive petitioners of their legitimes","issue":"W/N Petitioners have a legal interest over the properties subject of the Deeds of Sale -NO","ruling":"The SC ruled that under Art. 777 of the Civil Code, successional rights are transmitted only from the moment of death of the decedent, rendering a compulsory heir's right to the parents' estate merely inchoate and a bare expectancy during the parents' lifetime. Furthermore, under Art. 908, the legitime of a compulsory heir is computed only as of the time of the decedent's death; hence, compulsory heirs cannot claim an impairment of their legitime while their parents are still alive, nor do they possess the requisite legal interest as real parties-in-interest to question such inter vivos dispositions. Additionally, the sale of the lots to their siblings does not affect the value of their parents' estate. While the sale of the lots reduced the estate, cash of equivalent value replaced the lots taken from the estate.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 10–11; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"manongsong":{"name":"Manongsong v. Estimo","citation":"G.R. No. 136773 (2003), as stated in reviewer","doctrine":"A valid disposition inter vivos for valuable consideration does not impair the legitime of compulsory heirs because it does not diminish the seller’s estate. There is merely a substitution of values: the property sold is replaced by the equivalent monetary consideration. An heir claiming ownership by succession must first prove that the property actually formed part of the decedent’s estate.","facts":"Justina Navarro was the original owner of a parcel of land in Las Piñas. Agatona Guevarra and her husband had six children, including Enriqueta Lopez-Jumaquio and Vicente Lopez. Milagros Manongsong was Vicente’s daughter. Milagros filed an action for partition, claiming that the property belonged to Agatona and, upon Agatona’s death, passed to her children. Since one child died without offspring, Milagros claimed that Vicente became entitled to a 1/5 share, which she later inherited. The Jumaquio sisters, Enriqueta’s daughters, opposed the claim and presented a notarized 1957 Kasulatan sa Bilihan ng Lupa showing that Justina had sold the property to Enriqueta for ₱250.","issue":"W/N Milagros acquired a co-ownership interest in the property by succession and was therefore entitled to demand partition - NO","ruling":"Milagros failed to prove that the property ever formed part of Agatona’s estate. The notarized Kasulatan established that Justina sold the property to Enriqueta for valuable consideration. The RTC erred in treating the sale as invalid for allegedly depriving Agatona and her descendants of their legitimes. Unlike a gratuitous disposition, a valid sale for valuable consideration does not diminish the seller’s estate because the property is replaced by its monetary equivalent. Thus, the property did not pass to Agatona’s descendants by succession. Since Milagros failed to establish that she inherited any interest in the property, she was not a co-owner and had no right to demand partition.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 11–12; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"locsin":{"name":"Locsin v. Court of Appeals","citation":"G.R. No. ### (YYYY), placeholder in reviewer","doctrine":"The rights to a person's succession are transmitted from the moment of his death, and do not vest in her heirs until such time. Property which the testator conveyed to other persons during her lifetime no longer formed part of her estate at the time of her death to which her heirs may lay claim. Had she died intestate, only the property that remained in her estate at the time of her death devolved to her legal heirs. Only compulsory heirs are entitled to legitime which could be impaired by any transfer of property during the testator’s lifetime.","facts":"Don Mariano Locsin and Doña Catalina Jaucian were a childless couple who mutually agreed during their lifetime that their respective properties would eventually revert to their respective blood relatives. Decades after Don Mariano’s death in 1948, Doña Catalina transferred various real properties (from her husband's estate as well as her own) to Don Mariano's nephews and nieces (the Locsins) and to her own relatives. 6 years after Doña Catalina’s death in 1977, several of her Jaucian nephews and nieces filed a lawsuit to annul the conveyances and recover the properties.","issue":"W/N the private respondents (collateral Jaucian nephews and nieces) are entitled to inherit or recover properties which Doña Catalina had already disposed of during her lifetime. - NO","ruling":"The Supreme Court ruled in favor of the Locsins, holding that properties transferred inter vivos during the decedent's lifetime no longer form part of her hereditary estate upon death. Furthermore, collateral relatives are not compulsory heirs and have no legitimes; thus, Doña Catalina had complete freedom of disposition, and the action to annul had long prescribed..","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 11; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"calimag":{"name":"Calimag v. Heirs of Macapaz","citation":"G.R. No. 191936 (2016)","doctrine":"Under the Civil Code and the Rules on Evidence, the legitimate filiation of children entitled to succeed as legal heirs may be established by public documents such as certificates of live birth indicating the parents' marriage and open cohabitation as husband and wife, which carry the strong presumption of a valid marriage (semper praesumitur pro matrimonio). Consequently, the father's signature is not required on a legitimate child's birth certificate to prove legitimate filiation and the right to inherit ab intestato, rendering the bar under Article 992 inapplicable.","facts":"Respondents Anastacio Jr. and Alicia filed an action to annul a deed of sale and cancel a title fraudulently obtained by petitioner Calimag over land co-owned by their deceased aunt, Silvestra. Calimag challenged their legal capacity to sue as Silvestra's heirs, arguing under Article 992 of the Civil Code that they were illegitimate children because their birth certificates were signed only by their mother and lacked proof of a valid parental marriage.","issue":"W/N the respondents established their legitimate filiation to Silvestra's brother, thereby possessing the legal capacity to inherit from Silvestra's estate as her legal heirs - YES","ruling":"The respondents' NSO-issued certificates of live birth, which stated their parents' marriage date and place, constitute competent prima facie evidence of legitimate filiation and trigger the legal presumption of a valid marriage between their parents. Unlike registrations of illegitimate children, the law requires only either parent to declare the facts of birth for a legitimate child, fully confirming respondents' status as legitimate descendants and legal heirs with the personality to institute the action..","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 11–12; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"dolar":{"name":"In re: Will of Diancin / Dolar v. Bishop of Jaro","citation":"G.R. No. 46521 (1939)","doctrine":"There being forced heirs, the legacy of P8,000 should be taken from the free third only, without touching the obligatory legitime (article 813 of the Civil Code), and for this purpose, the properties to be partitioned should first be divided into three equal parts, two of which constitute the obligatory legitime, and the other the free third, so as to determine the properties from which the legacy should be taken.","facts":"Paulino Diancin acquired properties during his first and second marriages and, in his will, distributed them among his widow, Teopista Dolar, and his children from both marriages. He also left a P8,000 legacy to the Church of Dumangas, to be taken from the fruits of his properties before partition. After his death, testamentary proceedings were initiated, and Teopista was appointed judicial administratrix, but the first and second proposed partitions were both disapproved due to objections from the heirs and the Church’s representative, the Bishop of Jaro.","issue":"WON the payment of the P8,000 legacy from the products of all properties before liquidating conjugal partnerships and setting aside the obligatory legitime of forced heirs is proper. – NO.","ruling":"The Court ruled that the estate should first be settled by paying all debts and administration expenses and liquidating the conjugal properties from both marriages to determine the shares belonging to the deceased, his first-marriage heirs, and his widow Teopista Dolar. After settlement, the deceased’s properties would constitute his estate and be partitioned among his children from both marriages and Teopista. Since there were compulsory heirs, the P8,000 legacy could only be taken from the portion freely disposable by the testator and could not impair the heirs’ legitimes. Because the legacy was in usufruct, the heirs could instead deliver properties equivalent to the disposable portion, while only the corresponding portion of the fruits would answer for the legacy. Teopista’s legal usufruct would likewise be taken from the portion available for betterment. After partition, the compulsory heirs would receive their respective legitimes and the undisposed portion would belong to them equally, subject to the legacy as to its fruits.","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes, p. 12; wording transcribed from supplied reviewer","figures":[{"src":"assets/figures/diancin-estate-diagram.png","caption":"Original relationship/estate diagram — Succession Block Notes, p. 12","alt":"Family relationship or estate diagram included in the reviewer for In re: Will of Diancin / Dolar v. Bishop of Jaro"}],"source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"pecson":{"name":"Pecson v. Mediavillo","citation":"as cited in Jurado & Paras, Succession Block Notes p. 14","doctrine":null,"facts":"Florencio Pecson disinherited his granddaughter Rosario Mediavillo because she was “grossly disrespectful” and “on one occasion” raised her hand against him. She was about 14 then and became insane very soon after.","issue":null,"ruling":"The clause was annulled. A girl of 14 who shortly afterward became insane “was not responsible for her acts.” Maltreatment must be intentional and done with discernment. Acts by a child of tender years or one who is insane do not qualify. [Jurado; Paras]","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes p. 14; the reviewer labels these sections “Facts” and “Held”","source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"},"javier":{"name":"Javier v. Lucero","citation":"as cited in Jurado & Paras, Succession Block Notes p. 15","doctrine":null,"facts":"Salud Arca won a support order against her husband Alfredo Javier, who had obtained an Alabama divorce in 1941 and in 1950 married again in Manila. At her instance he was charged with bigamy and acquitted for lack of criminal intent, since he believed the divorce had dissolved his first marriage. He argued that she had forfeited support because her accusation was found false.","issue":null,"ruling":"No. Such an acquittal “is no different from an acquittal on reasonable doubt,” so the accusation was not “found to be false” (Arts. 303, 921). Petition denied. The acquittal must rest on the offense not having been committed. [Jurado; Paras]","ratio":null,"source":"Succession Block Notes p. 15; the reviewer labels these sections “Facts” and “Held”","source_type":"reviewer_transcription","source_label":"Original Succession reviewer text (line wrapping normalized)"}}}};
